-- The target language of the extractor: a small Java fragment, defined as an
-- abstract syntax tree in Arend.
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS. `ToJava.ard` used to build Java *source text* directly
-- (`String` concatenation), so the target language existed only implicitly, in
-- the shape of those string literals. This file makes it explicit:
--
--   * it IS the definition of the Java subset we commit to generating -- the
--     generator can only produce what these constructors allow;
--   * it is the object a future operational semantics (and hence any
--     correctness statement about the backend) will be given for;
--   * it separates "which Java construct" from "how it is spelled": printing is
--     one trusted function, `JavaPrint.ard`'s `printClass`, and the generator
--     no longer worries about parentheses, semicolons or whitespace.
--
-- SCOPE. Deliberately exactly what `compileExpr`/`compileDecl` emit, no
-- more: no arithmetic operators, no loops, no conditionals, no visibility
-- modifiers besides `public static`, no generics, no interfaces or class
-- declarations other than the single generated program class. Generated code is
-- untyped-style: every value has static type `Object` and is cast where needed,
-- so this fragment is NOT Featherweight Java and is not meant to be.
-- The fixed runtime (`Rt.Fn`, `Rt.Data`, `Rt.BOX`, `Rt.PRIM_*`) is hand-written
-- (`runtime/Rt.java`) and appears here only as referenced names, never as
-- declarations.
--
-- COMPARE (rationale and the longer survey live in notes/RESEARCH_AND_PLAN.md):
--   * Malfunction.v (metarocq-malfunction) -- the verified λ□ backend; its
--     `Ident.t := string` / `Longident.t := string` split is `JIdent`/`JQIdent`
--     below, and its `Mvar`/`Mglobal` split is `jVar`/`jStatic`.
--   * CompCert `cfrontend/Clight.v` -- the same two-sort `expr`/`statement`
--     shape (`Ecast`, `Efield`, `Sassign`, `Sswitch`, `Sreturn`), and the same
--     "target AST in the proof assistant, printer outside it" division that
--     CertiCoq's C backend uses (`printClight.ml`).
--   * Featherweight Java (Igarashi-Pierce-Wadler) -- `x | e.f | e.m(e*) |
--     new C(e*) | (C)e` is exactly the expression core here; our extra nodes
--     (literals, arrays, assignment, statements) are the ones a code generator
--     cannot avoid.
--   * Jinja / µJava (Isabelle) -- the existing Java-source semantics, and the
--     dissenting design: Jinja folds statements into expressions, we do not.
--
-- IDENTIFIERS. Two type synonyms, both `String`: `JIdent` for a simple Java
-- identifier and `JQIdent` for a possibly dotted (qualified) one. Every such
-- position holds an ALREADY-VALID Java name: mangling happens at exactly one
-- boundary (`mangleKername` in `ToJava.ard`), and generator-invented binders
-- (`p<path>`, `l<path>`, ...) are legal by construction. Plain strings are what
-- every comparable *source-level* backend uses (MetaRocq `ident := string`,
-- lean-to-lambdabox `abbrev Ident := String`, peregrine's sanitizers
-- `utf8_string -> string`); interned integers (CompCert `ident := positive`,
-- CertiCoq λANF `var`) buy cheap maps and provable freshness, neither of which
-- this generator needs.
--
-- STATEMENTS AND EXPRESSIONS ARE TWO SORTS, mutually recursive (a statement
-- contains expressions; an anonymous-class expression contains statements).
-- That mirrors the generator's `Out = \Sigma String String`, a statement
-- prelude plus a value expression; after the retarget it becomes
-- `\Sigma (Array JStmt) JExpr`.
--
-- BINDERS ARE NAMES, not de Bruijn indices: the `path`-derived names are what
-- makes the generator's recursion pass Arend's termination checker without
-- state threading. Well-scopedness can be added later (closure conversion is
-- the natural point).
--
-- COMMENTS are trivia, and they are available in *every* position that
-- occupies a line of its own -- statements (`jComment`) and class members
-- (`jMemberComment`) -- rather than only where the generator happens to need
-- one today. Expressions and types carry no comment node: they have no line to
-- attach one to, and comment-as-expression would have to survive printing in
-- the middle of a term. Nothing else in the AST is annotatable, so this is
-- "everywhere possible".

\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Identifiers
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A simple Java identifier: a local, a parameter, a field or a method name.
\func JIdent => String

-- A possibly dotted Java name: `Rt`, `Rt.Fn`, `System.out`,
-- `java.math.BigInteger`, `Long.valueOf`.
\func JQIdent => String

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Types
--
-- Only what appears in generated code: `Object` (everything), a named class or
-- interface (`Rt.Fn`, `Rt.Data`, `java.math.BigInteger`, `String`), arrays
-- (`Object[]`, `Rt.Fn[]`), and `void` (only `main`'s return type).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data JType
  | tObject
  | tVoid
  | tClass JQIdent
  | tArray JType

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Expressions
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data JExpr
  -- A local variable or method parameter used as a value: `p3`, `l7`.
  -- (Malfunction's `Mvar`, Clight's `Evar`, Featherweight Java's `x`.)
  | jVar JIdent
  -- A qualified name used as a value or as the receiver of a static member:
  -- `Rt`, `System.out`. Kept apart from `jVar` because it is resolved
  -- globally, not in the local environment (Malfunction's `Mglobal`).
  | jStatic JQIdent
  -- `e.f` -- field access: `d.tag`, `d.fields`, `Rt.BOX`, `Rt.PRIM_ADD_INT`.
  | jField JExpr JIdent
  -- `e[i]` -- array access: `d.fields[2]`, `x3[1]`.
  -- There is no arithmetic node, so the one addition the generator used to emit
  -- (`fields[npars + fieldIdx]` for `proj`) is folded on the Arend side into a
  -- single `jIntLit`.
  | jIndex JExpr JExpr
  -- `(T)(e)` -- a downcast; unavoidable, since every value is typed `Object`.
  | jCast JType JExpr
  -- `e.m(args)` -- instance method call: `.apply(a)`, `System.out.println(..)`.
  | jInvoke JExpr JIdent (Array JExpr)
  -- `m(args)` where `m` may be qualified: a generated constant `c_foo()`,
  -- `Rt.unbound(i, path)`, `Long.valueOf(5L)`.
  | jCallStatic JQIdent (Array JExpr)
  -- `new T(args)` -- `new Rt.Data(tag, fields)`, `new java.math.BigInteger("7")`,
  -- `new RuntimeException("no matching branch")`.
  | jNew JType (Array JExpr)
  -- `new T[]{ es }` -- an array with an initializer: `new Object[]{ a, b }`.
  | jNewArrayInit JType (Array JExpr)
  -- `new T[n]` -- a sized (null-filled, later assigned) array: the `Rt.Fn[k]`
  -- used to tie the knot for `fix`.
  | jNewArraySized JType JExpr
  -- An anonymous class implementing a one-method interface, i.e. our closures:
  --   `new <iface>(){ public Object apply(Object <param>){ <body> } }`
  -- The method is not a `JMethod` here but spelled out as (interface, parameter
  -- name, body): it is always `public Object apply(Object)`, and a record field
  -- would put `JMethod` into the JExpr/JStmt recursion, which Arend rejects
  -- (records cannot be part of a mutual `\data` cycle).
  -- This node is temporary by design: closure conversion (step 5) replaces it
  -- with named `Closure_<path>` classes.
  | jAnonFn JType JIdent (Array JStmt)
  -- A `String` literal; the printer is responsible for quoting and escaping.
  | jStringLit String
  -- A decimal `int` literal: constructor tags, field indices, array indices,
  -- array sizes. Nat, because every such number is a position or a count.
  | jIntLit Nat
  -- A `long` literal (`5L`), i.e. `Int` because it may be negative. Only used
  -- to build λ□ `prim` int values (`Long.valueOf(5L)`); the `BigInteger`
  -- representation instead uses `jNew` over a `jStringLit`.
  | jLongLit Int

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Statements
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data JStmt
  -- A local variable declaration, optionally `final` and optionally
  -- initialized: `final Object l3 = e;`, `Object r3;`.
  -- An uninitialized one is exactly the `case` result variable, assigned in
  -- every branch.
  | jLocal (isFinal : Bool) JType JIdent (Maybe JExpr)
  -- `lhs = rhs;` where `lhs` is a name or an array element (the `fix` knot).
  | jAssign JExpr JExpr
  -- `switch (scrut) { cases default: dflt }`, where a case is
  -- `case <tag>: { <stmts> break; }` -- the printer emits the block and the
  -- `break`, so this fragment has no fallthrough. The cases are a `\Sigma`
  -- rather than a record for the same reason as `jAnonFn` above (and as
  -- `LambdaBox.ard`'s `case` branches); Malfunction's
  -- `Mswitch of t * (case list * t) list` makes the same choice.
  | jSwitch JExpr (Array (\Sigma Nat (Array JStmt))) (Array JStmt)
  -- `throw e;`
  | jThrow JExpr
  -- `return e;`
  | jReturn JExpr
  -- An expression evaluated for its effect only: `System.out.println(..);`.
  | jExprStmt JExpr
  -- `// <text>` on a line of its own; see COMMENTS above.
  | jComment String


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Members and the program class
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\record JParam (paramType : JType) (paramName : JIdent)

-- A method of the generated class: a constant `c_foo`, `__main`, or `main`.
-- (The `apply` of a closure is part of `jAnonFn` instead, see above.)
\record JMethod
  (mIsStatic : Bool)
  (mReturn : JType)
  (mName : JIdent)
  (mParams : Array JParam)
  (mBody : Array JStmt)

\data JMember
  | jMethod JMethod
  -- `// <text>` in class-body position: what an erased inductive produces.
  | jMemberComment String

-- The single generated class (`public final class Prog { ... }`); the runtime
-- class is hand-written and not part of this AST.
\record JClass (className : JIdent) (classMembers : Array JMember)
